;;; Ein stdlib — `std.core`: the rules every program gets.
;;;
;;; Every program gets this module with no import line: the loader brings it
;;; into every program, whole, after the program's own forms and those of
;;; the modules it imports (Q-M1f.551). A file may import it as it imports
;;; any module, which binds its names and brings no second copy, and a
;;; library `$EIN_STDLIB` names brings its own. Its rule is then a rule of
;;; the program as any other is: `ein saturate --dump` lists it under its
;;; full name, `std.core.inconsistent`, and a trace shows its firing.
;;;
;;; ── `(false)` is a proposition ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; `(false)` is a fact: a rule concludes it, a program states it, another
;;; fact holds it under an arrow, `(think Ann (false))`. What makes a state
;;; that holds it dead is no reading of its name. It is the rule below, which
;;; matches a held `(false)` and states `:ein inconsistent`, a word to the
;;; engine and no fact (Q-M1f.399, Q-M1f.550): where a rule that states it
;;; fires, the saturation of the state ends and the state is dead. The firing
;;; writes no fact.
;;;
;;; ── what an explanation says of it ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;; A dead state's core is the frontier of what the firing matched, the
;;; `(false)`, and `refuted by` names the rule that concluded that `(false)`:
;;; an explanation names the rules a program wrote or imported, and reads
;;; through a rule of this module to what its firing rests on.

(rule inconsistent ()
  :match (false)
  :ein   inconsistent
  :why   "a state that derives a contradiction is inconsistent")
